Style suggestion: Consider using a ternary or IIFE to avoid `let` with reassignment here. The file already uses IIFE patterns elsewhere (e.g., `cutoffTime`, `windowDays`). For example:

```ts
const sortedModels = (() => {
  const sorted = Object.entries(stats.modelUsage).sort(([, a], [, b]) => b.messages - a.messages)
  if (modelFilter && modelFilter.length > 0) {
    return sorted.filter(([model]) => modelFilter.some((filter) => model.includes(filter)))
  }
  return sorted
})()
```

After filtering out thinking blocks, if the rest array is empty, the assistant message will have an empty content array. Some APIs may reject messages with empty content. This scenario could occur if an assistant message had only thinking blocks in its content. Consider adding a check: if rest is empty after filtering, either skip the transformation for that message or add a placeholder text part to ensure the message has valid content.
```suggestion
              const reasoningContent = thinking.map((b: any) => b.thinking).join("")
              if (reasoningContent) {
                msg[field] = reasoningContent
              }
              // Ensure assistant messages always have at least one content block
              if (rest.length === 0) {
                rest.push({ type: "text", text: "" })
              }
```

In the non-Anthropic path, if all content parts are reasoning parts, the filtered array will be empty, resulting in an assistant message with an empty content array. Some APIs may reject messages with empty content. Consider adding a check: if filtered is empty, either skip the transformation or add a placeholder text part to ensure valid message structure.
```suggestion

        // In the non-Anthropic path, avoid emitting an assistant message
        // with an empty content array when all parts are reasoning.
        if (filtered.length === 0) {
          return msg
        }
```

When steer is not accepted (session is idle), the backend already creates a no-reply user message in the steer function (session/prompt.ts line 294-298). Calling prompt() again here will create a duplicate message with the same text. This code should be removed, and when accepted is false, the flow should simply set sent to true since the message was already persisted by the backend.
```suggestion
          // When steer is not accepted (session is idle), the backend already
          // creates a no-reply user message, so we simply mark this as sent
          // and avoid calling prompt() again to prevent duplicate messages.
          sent = true
```

The test name suggests that steer messages should be "drained in timestamp order", but the test only verifies that all three messages exist using `.toSorted()` which sorts the array for comparison. The test doesn't actually verify that the messages are stored or retrieved in timestamp order. Consider either:
1. Renaming the test to reflect that it only checks for existence of all messages
2. Adding assertions that verify the actual order matches the timestamp order (e.g., checking that the steer_timestamp values in the parts array are in ascending order)
```suggestion
        // verify that steer messages are drained in timestamp order
        const texts = parts.map((part) => part.text)
        expect(texts).toEqual(["first", "second", "third"])
        const sortedTimestamps = [...(timestamps as number[])].toSorted((a, b) => a - b)
        expect(timestamps).toEqual(sortedTimestamps)
```

The constructed bun download URL hard-codes `${OS}/x64` and derives `OS` by simply lowercasing `$RUNNER_OS`. This will break workflows that run this composite action on macOS (RUNNER_OS=macOS -> "macos", but Bun download URLs use "darwin") and on ARM runners (e.g. aarch64-linux / aarch64-darwin in the publish matrix), because the URL will point at the wrong platform/arch binary. Please map `RUNNER_OS` to Bun’s expected platform segment (macOS->darwin) and derive the arch segment from `$RUNNER_ARCH`/`uname -m` (X64->x64, ARM64->aarch64) instead of hard-coding x64.
```suggestion
        case "${RUNNER_OS:-}" in
          macOS) OS=darwin ;;
          Linux) OS=linux ;;
          Windows) OS=windows ;;
          *) OS=$(printf '%s' "$RUNNER_OS" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]') ;;
        esac

        ARCH_FROM_ENV="${RUNNER_ARCH:-}"
        case "$ARCH_FROM_ENV" in
          X64) ARCH=x64 ;;
          ARM64) ARCH=aarch64 ;;
          *)
            ARCH_UNAME=$(uname -m)
            case "$ARCH_UNAME" in
              x86_64) ARCH=x64 ;;
              arm64|aarch64) ARCH=aarch64 ;;
              *) ARCH="$ARCH_UNAME" ;;
            esac
            ;;
        esac

        echo "url=https://bun.sh/download/${V}/${OS}/${ARCH}?avx2=false&profile=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
```

The cleanup loop always forces a full GC and sleeps 100ms even on non-Windows platforms (where EBUSY is unlikely). This adds a fixed delay and extra GC pressure to every test run; consider gating the GC+retry logic behind `process.platform === "win32"` (or similar) and using a single `fs.rm` on other platforms.
```suggestion

  if (process.platform === "win32") {
    const busy = (error: unknown) =>
      typeof error === "object" && error !== null && "code" in error && (error as { code?: unknown }).code === "EBUSY"
    const rm = async (left: number): Promise<void> => {
      Bun.gc(true)
      await Bun.sleep(100)
      return fs.rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true }).catch((error) => {
        if (!busy(error)) throw error
        if (left <= 1) throw error
        return rm(left - 1)
      })
    }

    // Windows can keep SQLite WAL handles alive until GC finalizers run, so we
    // force GC and retry teardown to avoid flaky EBUSY in test cleanup.
    await rm(30)
  } else {
    // On non-Windows platforms, EBUSY is unlikely; a single rm is sufficient.
    await fs.rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }
```

