The registry value parsing on line 342 has a potential bug. If the registry type appears at the end of the line (index is the last element), then tokens[index + 1..] will be an empty slice, and joining it will produce an empty string. This could happen with malformed registry output. Consider adding a bounds check to ensure index + 1 is less than tokens.len() before attempting to join the remaining tokens.
```suggestion

                if index + 1 >= tokens.len() {
                    // Malformed line: registry type appears at the end with no value.
                    continue;
                }
```

The resolve_cmd function reads the entire file into memory (line 382-383). For large batch or cmd files, this could consume significant memory. While most batch files are small, consider adding a size check before reading or using a streaming approach for large files to prevent potential memory issues or DoS if a malicious user can control the path resolution.
```suggestion
    let resolve_cmd = |path: &Path| -> Option<String> {
        // Avoid reading arbitrarily large files into memory to prevent potential DoS.
        // Most batch/cmd files are small, so we enforce a reasonable upper limit.
        const MAX_CMD_FILE_SIZE: u64 = 10 * 1024 * 1024; // 10 MiB
        let metadata = std::fs::metadata(path).ok()?;
        if metadata.len() > MAX_CMD_FILE_SIZE {
            return None;
        }
```

The textColor and focusedTextColor changes remove the visual feedback when the leader key is active. Previously, these colors would change to theme.textMuted when keybind.leader was true, consistent with how the model name text changes color on line 1007. This appears to be an unintended change that's unrelated to the width fix. Consider restoring the conditional color logic: `textColor={keybind.leader ? theme.textMuted : theme.text}` and `focusedTextColor={keybind.leader ? theme.textMuted : theme.text}` to maintain consistent leader key visual feedback throughout the prompt UI.
```suggestion
              textColor={keybind.leader ? theme.textMuted : theme.text}
              focusedTextColor={keybind.leader ? theme.textMuted : theme.text}
```

The extractAllCodeBlocks function assumes match(FENCE_CLOSE) will always succeed after checking `end >= 0`, but this could fail if the regex doesn't match. Line 68 uses the non-null assertion operator (!) which will throw if match returns null. Add a null check before accessing match[0].
```suggestion
    const afterEnd = rest.slice(end)
    const closeMatch = afterEnd.match(FENCE_CLOSE)
    if (!closeMatch) {
      // Fallback: no closing fence match found where expected; treat the remainder as plain text.
      remaining = afterEnd
    } else {
      remaining = rest.slice(end + closeMatch[0].length)
    }
```
